\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc{\loch\fs18\i\loch
Figure 3. Tag window. Tagging options are inherited from the main window and reported in Review Operation.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
The standalone and GUI Tagger write tags directly to the selected Tagging Path unless the main-window Dry run checkbox is selected. The Tag window has no option checkboxes copied from the main window and no separate inherited-settings notice. It contains the Tagging Path plus Tag, Pause or Resume, and Quit controls. When Tag is clicked, it reads the current main-window values for Compliant, etreeDB, setlist.fm, setlist.fm upgrade, Thorough Setlist Matching, Rename Compliantly, Convert shn, Artist in Album Tag, As-Is Artist Name, and Dry run. The inventory Tag in Place, Tag Copy, and Tag Copy/Delete Original values are also reported in Review Operation, but standalone Tag always tags the selected path directly and never copies or moves folders. When Dry run is on, clicking Tag resolves the show metadata and track titles without writing tags, converting SHN, renaming, or deleting files. The output lists each applicable audio file with the exact Artist, Album, Track, and Title values that would be written, the title source, planned SHN-to-FLAC targets, and any reason a file would be skipped.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
Before an actual tag save, TLO compares the exact normalized Artist, Album, Title, and Track Number it intends to write with the current file. When those values already match and no stale TRACKTOTAL, DISCNUMBER, or DISCTOTAL metadata needs removal, the file is reported unchanged and is not rewritten. A difference in any written value, an extra value that the write would replace, or stale total/disc metadata still causes the normal write and cleanup. Rename Compliantly uses the same no-op principle: Full Inventory, Add Shows, and the Tagger do not call the filesystem rename operation when the final compliant folder name is already the effective current name.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
The Tagger does not copy folders and does not delete music content. The only exception is Convert shn: after a replacement FLAC is successfully created and verified, TLO deletes the source .shn or .shnf file. Failed conversions leave the source file in place.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
On native Windows installations, drag a folder from File Explorer and drop it onto the Tagging Path field in the tagger window. The dropped folder replaces the Tagging Path value. If a file is dropped, TLO uses the containing folder. This tagger drag-and-drop support is not enabled for WSL, Linux, or macOS.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
Track-title safety: when a numbered setlist begins with a bare track number such as 01 and the next meaningful numbered song row confirms 02, TLO keeps track 01 with Title Unknown and continues the numbered list. A short numeric-only row cannot start an unstructured unnumbered-title block; numeric song names such as 69 and 1999 remain valid when embedded inside an established song block. Compact personnel credits such as Gary Burton(vib) or Chick Corea (p), and duration summaries such as min 38:24 are not unnumbered song titles. A convincing run of at least three consecutive numbered song rows still blocks arbitrary later prose from being scavenged merely because its line count matches the audio files. Before a shorter numbered candidate is padded with Unknown titles, however, TLO now checks for a plausible unnumbered song-list candidate whose count exactly matches the audio files. If numbered and unnumbered candidates both match the audio count, matching audio filenames and existing Title tags only reinforce a candidate; missing or unrelated filename/tag values are neutral. A short numbered block embedded in surrounding collector prose may lose an otherwise unsupported equal-count tie to a contiguous unnumbered song block. For an explicit Setlist:, Songs:, or Tracks: section containing one song per line, Notes: and other track-list terminators end the song section before later numbered collector notes can be interpreted as tracks. Legitimate titles beginning with Thank remain valid; prose such as Thanks to the taper remains a terminator. E:, Enc:, and Encore: prefixes are removed from an encore song title. Multi-disc filenames that include both disc and track tokens, including punctuation-separated forms such as CJM-Disc01,Track01.flac, are ordered by disc first and track second so disc 1 completes before disc 2 and written Track Number tags do not alternate odd/even between discs. The same ordering applies to validated multipart release folders named Parent (1) through Parent (N): the parenthesized part number is ordered first and each folder's filename track number second, so part 1 completes before part 2 and written Track Number tags do not stripe all track 01 files across the parts. Explicit Set 1, Disc 1, Disk 1, CD 1, Setlist 1, Set List 1, Track List 1, Songs:, and Tracks: headings mark the beginning of an unnumbered song region. When one is present, artist/venue/lineage text above the first boundary is not eligible as song titles just because adding those lines would make the count match the audio files. Blank lines, later Set/Disc/Disk/CD boundaries, and a bare Encore heading can continue the same ordered region. A date-labelled filler divider such as on July 7, 1967: is skipped as a title while following songs remain eligible. Notes/source/checksum/technical markers and decorative separators end the region. Valid date-prefixed broadcast/show dividers with trailing context, for example 30.10.1948 - Royal Roost, NY - WMCA Radio broadcast, are section boundaries and are not track 30. In an established numbered run, if TLO provisionally sees a higher number but the next numbered row resumes the exact number that was expected, the higher row is discarded as a false positive; 01-07, a date-like 30 row, then 08 therefore continues with track 08. Confirmed large gaps remain valid when later numbering reinforces them, such as 01, 02, 30, 31. Artist/header safety: a DB artist word embedded only as the trailing city of a longer path location such as Washington, DC is not used as generic path artist evidence, although an exact artist directory named Washington remains valid. Structured unlabeled headers may contain Artist / room-or-hall / named Venue / Location / Date; TLO keeps the first line as artist and the deeper named venue as Venue. A collaboration such as Chick Corea and Herbie Hancock may be accepted without a combined Artist DB row only when every component resolves uniquely in the Artist DB. Bare-number title safety: when one contiguous bare-number block exactly matches the audio-file count, the numbers are treated as track positions and the Titles are Unknown; the numeric values themselves are never written as song titles.\line The tagger also creates CorruptFlacs.txt in TLOHome. If a FLAC file cannot be opened or written during tagging, TLO appends the complete file path to that file so bad FLACs can be reviewed separately from other tagging errors. Exact placeholder titles Title, Titled, or Titles become Unknown after normal cleanup, including numbered forms such as 01. Title; longer legitimate titles containing those words are left unchanged. Machine-generated extraction/analysis tables are never accepted as song lists merely because their rows form a clean numeric sequence. Exact Audio Copy (EAC) TOC/extraction rows and other timestamp/sector-style multi-column records are technical data, and EAC markers start a technical tail that is not mined for titles. An explicit Songs: or Tracks: block is still retained as real local list evidence even when its count differs from the audio-file count; TLO reports the mismatch instead of truncating, shifting, or inventing a one-to-one mapping. When an apostrophe-style duration is accidentally joined to a valid dated subsection heading because of a lost newline, TLO can retain the song row while removing the attached dated-divider text.}
